[CourseColor "white"]
[CourseDescription "An excellent opening for beginners and intermediates, relying on an early central attack. Pair with the Fried Liver course."]
[Chapter "The c3 and d4 Italian"]
[Study "Black Plays 7...Nxe4 (Main Line)"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/Italian Center Attack/01-01 The c3 and d4 Italian - Black Plays 7...Nxe4 (Main Line).mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{Welcome to the Italian! Let's take a look at the Italian Game, where Black brings out the bishop to c5 on the third move (instead of 3...Nf6, allowing us to go for The Fried Liver.) The following four studies explore White's c3, d4 setup. Let's check it out!}
1. e4 e5 {There won't be an Italian game without 1.e4 and 1...e5 on the board! As usual, our knight will occupy its usual f3-square, attacking e5 and controlling the center.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Black develops in a similar way, supporting the pawn on e5. Next, we will play 3.Bc4 — the signature move of the Italian! [%csl Re5]} 3. Bc4 Bc5 {3...Bc5 is the second most popular move in this position (after ...Nf6), played in nearly 30% of the games. Black doesn't allow us to go for the Fried Liver Attack, but instead brings the bishop out to c5, with similar intentions as our bishop on c4. Our recommendation in this course is to immediately play 4.c3, initiating a simple, but powerful idea of a quick d4-push.} 4. c3 Nf6 {Nearly 60% of the games continue with Black bringing the knight to f6. Now it's time for d4. Note that 5.d3 defending the e-pawn is perfectly playable, but we will be looking at the more forcing and aggressive 5.d4 in this course. [%csl Re4]} 5. d4 exd4 {Black should sensibly trade on d4, as other moves are significantly weaker. This occurs in 90% of the games. We recapture with the pawn, which comes with an attack on the c5-bishop while maintaining our pawn center.} 6. cxd4 Bb4+ {The following studies are divided in regards to what Black does in this moment and on the next move. First, we will look at Bb4+. Black gives a check on b4, which is a move that has been played 75% of the time. We recommend 7.Nc3 here, a very sharp and active approach that doesn't block our dark-squared bishop. [%csl Re1]} 7. Nc3 Nxe4 {Now, 7...Nxe4 is by far the strongest move for Black and also the most common, played almost 60% of the time. Black captures a pawn and piles up on the c3-knight, but that's what we use to our advantage! While Black is occupied by the knight on c3, our king gets to safety and prepares Re1+ against the uncastled king! [%csl Rc3]} 8. O-O Nxc3 {This capture occurs close to 45% of the time. Black is likely hoping to relieve the central tension without giving up their bishop pair. We will simply recapture the knight.} (8... Bxc3 {In 47% of the games, Black captures on c3 with the bishop. We can now gain some space in the center with 9.d5, attacking the knight at the same time.} 9. d5 Bf6 {Many times, Black saves the bishop with ...Bf6. Let's line up our rook with the uncastled king. [%csl Re8]} (9... Ne5 {Black immediately saves the knight by jumping to e5 and counter-attacking our c4-bishop. We'll grab the bishop on c3. [%csl Rc4,Rc3]} 10. bxc3 Nxc4 {In return, Black gets our light-squared bishop. To continue playing actively, our next move is Qd4. From d4, the queen has three targets: the pawn on g7 and the knights on e4 and c4.} 11. Qd4 Ncd6 {Black saves the knights by retreating to d6. We can now grab the g7-pawn and attack the rook on h8. [%csl Rg7]} 12. Qxg7 Qf6 {Black defends with ...Qf8. The queen trade is more or less forced here, as there are no real safe squares for our queen (13.Qg4? runs into ...h5!) After Black recaptures, we'll be able to generate counterplay with Re1+.} 13. Qxf6 Nxf6 {Black recaptures on f6 with the knight. Let's give the check we were planning! The lack of Black's dark-squared bishop is apparent here, and Black's queenside is also heavily underdeveloped.} 14. Re1+ Kf8 {...Kf8 is a huge mistake, but played nearly a whopping 70% of the time! We can play Bh6+! to set up a mating net.} (14... Nfe4 {This is the only move which keeps equality, but it is played only in 14% of the games. We'll add pressure to this pinned knight. [%csl Re4,Re8]} 15. Nd2 f5 {Black supports the knight with ...f5. [%csl Gf5,Ge4]} 16. f3 {We'll regain the piece. After a few trades on e4, White will be left with a slightly better bishop than Black. But it's nearly an endgame with opposite-colored bishops, so this position maintains its equality. [%cal Gd2e4,Gf3e4,Ge1e4] [%csl Gc1,Rc8]}) 15. Bh6+ Kg8 {The only move.} 16. Re5 {And Rg5+ is a serious threat, which is hard to defend. Black could go for ...Ne4 to stop Rg5+, but then we have Nh4 with f3 coming to kick it out. [%cal Gf3h4,Gf2f3,Gd6e4,Re5g5] [%csl Rg8]}) 10. Re1 Ne7 {Black saves the knight on c6. White simply recaptures on e4.} 11. Rxe4 {Now we have a handful of attacking ideas. Obviously Black is quite solid and smart to reach this far though, so a fight is ahead.}) 9. bxc3 Bxc3 {Black grabs on c3 yet again and attacks our rook on a1: a very natural-looking move. However, ...Bxc3? actually gives us a big advantage! First, let's prevent Black from castling by playing Ba3. [%csl Ra1]} (9... Be7 {Black plays it "safely" and retreats back to e7. Now we can gain space with d5 and kick the knight out from c6.} 10. d5 Na5 {Black jumps to a5, attacking our bishop. Our primary goal should be to open up the e-file against Black's uncastled king. The key move here is thus 11.d6!, also opening up our light-squared bishop, aimed at the soft f7-pawn. [%csl Rc4]} 11. d6 cxd6 {Black grabs the pawn on d6. Now, we can sacrifice our bishop on f7! The purpose of this sacrifice is to lure the king out into the open; Black's minor pieces are very passive, which usually indicates that a piece sacrifice is a correct one in the position. [%csl Rf7]} 12. Bxf7+ Kxf7 {Black recaptures. Let's bring the queen into the attack with check!} 13. Qd5+ Kf8 {The king retreats to f8. Now for another key move: we bring the dark-squared bishop to g5, further pressuring Black's pieces and allowing the rook on a1 to join the attack as well.} 14. Bg5 Nc6 {Black supports the bishop on e7 by retreating the knight. The knight was also loose on a5, leading to possible tactics if Black's queen was deflected. It is time to bring the a1 rook into the game.} 15. Rae1 Bxg5 {Black gets rid of the pressure on the bishop by trading it off. Of course, we would never trade queens here. We'll recapture with the knight, which comes with a checkmate threat. [%cal Gf3g5] [%csl Rf7]} 16. Nxg5 Qf6 {The queen prevents us from delivering a checkmate. Re3! is a strong rook lift, threatening Rf3 to pin the queen to the king.} 17. Re3 Ne5 {Black prevents Rf3.} 18. f4 {f4 kicks the knight out from e5. After the knight moves, we'll double up the rooks on the e-file and continue hunting down the king! [%cal Gf1e1,Re1e8] [%csl Ge3,Ge1,Re5]}) (9... d5 {This is the engine's top choice, equalizing the position. We next improve the rook by giving a check on e1. [%csl Rc4]} 10. Re1+ Be7 {Black saves the bishop and blocks the check. We'll save our bishop as well. [%csl Re1]} (10... Be6 {Black keeps the tension on the queenside and blocks the check by playing ...Be6. This is a mistake, yet it happened in 22% of the games! Here, we should gladly grab the bishop on b4! [%cal Rd5c4] [%csl Rc4]} 11. cxb4 dxc4 {Black thinks they've got out of trouble.} 12. d5 {But now we deliver a fork on d5 and win a piece! [%cal Gd5c6,Gd5e6] [%csl Re1,Re8]}) 11. Bd3 {This position is equal. Nevertheless, both of our bishops have good diagonals and we have the semi-open b and e-files to work with. White has good compensation for the pawn. [%cal Gd3h7,Gc1d2,Ga1b1,Gh2h3,Rc8g4]}) 10. Ba3 Bxa1 {Black's greed doesn't end here and they even capture the rook! It is time to punish Black's uncastled king and lack of development. White can start by giving a check with the remaining rook. [%csl Re8]} (10... d6 {Black blocks the scope of the dark-squared bishop on a3 and intends to castle. We'll save our rook with tempo, attacking the bishop.} 11. Rc1 Ba5 {Black retreats to a5. Now, a key move: our queen can add pressure to the knight and the bishop at the same time.} 12. Qa4 d5 {We are threatening d4-d5, so Black attacks our bishop in return while blocking our d-pawn. However, 13.Bb5 comes and Black's pieces are loose! [%csl Rc4]} 13. Bb5 {Following ...Bb6, Black's position will fall apart after Bxc6. [%cal Gb5c6,Ga4a5] [%csl Gc1,Rc6]}) 11. Re1+ Ne7 {Black can only block with a piece, since the king cannot go to f8. Let's grab the knight, which is attacked twice by a rook and bishop, but defended by a king and a queen! [%cal Ga3e7,Ge1e7] [%csl Re7]} 12. Rxe7+ Qxe7 {Black captures with the queen. Now it's time to capture the queen. [%csl Re7]} 13. Bxe7 {Here, once the bishops on e7 and a1 come off the board, the material is approximately equal by point count, but the position isn't equal at all. Our pieces are MUCH more active than Black's, and White's queen should coordinate with the minor pieces to wreak havoc against Black's open king. These factors should be decisive in this position. [%cal Gd1a1,Re8e7] [%csl Gh5,Ra8,Rh8,Rc8]} *

[Chapter "The c3 and d4 Italian"]
[Study "Black Plays 7...0-0"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/Italian Center Attack/01-02 The c3 and d4 Italian - Black Plays 7...0-0.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{The second study analyses 7...O-O. Black doesn't capture the pawn on e4, but gets their king into safety instead. Let's check it out!}
1. e4 e5 {As always, we begin with 1.e4 e5, and then look to develop normally.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Both knights are developed again, and now we will play 3.Bc4 to enter the Italian.} 3. Bc4 Bc5 {Again, 3...Bc5 develops and avoids the Fried Liver. As you saw in the last study, our recommendation here is to play for a quick c2-c3 and d2-d4 push.} 4. c3 Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported. [%csl Re4]} 5. d4 exd4 {Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6. cxd4 Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommend here is to block with Nc3. [%csl Re1]} 7. Nc3 O-O {This move is played about 15% of the time, and Black immediately moves the king out of the center. We'll do the same, temporarily sacrificing the pawn on e4, but the king's safety is more important here, as Black can have many tactics along the e-file. [%csl Re4]} 8. O-O Bxc3 {Black goes for the e4-pawn by trading away its defender. This is an intentional gambit on our part. We recapture the bishop. [%csl Re4]} 9. bxc3 Nxe4 {Black grabs the e4 pawn. Now, the thematic move: d5! We aggressively displace Black's knight, and also prevent Black from playing d5 themselves, which would solve a lot of their problems.} 10. d5 Na5 {The most popular move, occurs in nearly half of the games. Black tries to get rid of our strong light-squared bishop. We'll retreat it to d3 and counterattack the e4 knight. ...Nxc3, ...Nf6, and ...Nc5 are the most popular moves, and they all losing! Only ...f5 holds. [%cal Ra5c4] [%csl Rc4]} (10... Ne7 {In 39% of the games, Black retreats the knight to e7. White can add pressure along the e-file with their rook. [%csl Re7,Re4]} 11. Re1 Nd6 {In 60% of the the knight retreats to safety on d6 and attacks our bishop. However, this blocks the d-pawn, which hinders their development in the long term. We'll tuck it back the bishop, once again eyeing the h7-pawn. [%cal Rd6c4] [%csl Rd7,Rc8]} (11... Nf6 {In a quarter of the games, Black retreats the knight to f6. The thematic move when Black's knight is on f6 is Bg5; we add pressure to the knight, and here we threaten to double the f-pawns in front of Black's king.} 12. Bg5 Ng6 {Black supports the knight by opening up the queen, although this means the knight is pinned. Time to target the knight on g6 again! h2-h4 is the classic way to do this.} 13. h4 h6 {Black attacks our bishop in return. But we can just keep advancing! Black can't take on h5 because the f6 knight is pinned to the queen. [%csl Rg5]} 14. h5 hxg5 {Black takes the bishop which was pinning their f6-knight. We can take a piece too, but our capture has much graver strategic consequences for Black's position.} (14... Nh8 {Black tries to play defensively by retreating the knight back to h8.} 15. Bh4 {By retreating the bishop we maintain the pin on f6 and try to make use of Black's heavy underdevelopment. [%csl Rf6,Rh8,Rc8]}) 15. hxg6 fxg6 {Black recaptures on g6. The g6-pawn and the light-squared bishop had a nice interplay aimed at the f7-pawn. Now, we have an excellent discovered check to play. [%csl Rg8]} 16. d6+ Kh8 {The king moves off the diagonal.} 17. Ne5 {Black's position just collapses after our knight enters a strong outpost on e5, targeting the g6-pawn. The tripled isolated pawns are indefensible, and White's remaining pieces will enter the attack via the h-file. [%cal Ge5g6] [%csl Rg6]}) 12. Bd3 Ng6 {Black blocks the view of the bishop. Now, h4! is an aggressive move, intending to kick away he knight from g6.} 13. h4 Nxh4 {Black captures on h4, since their queen is also looking over that square. But the Greek Gift sacrifice works here as well! [%csl Rh7]} 14. Bxh7+ Kxh7 {Black captures. It's a simple pattern: we follow up with Ng5+ and then bring the queen into the attack.} 15. Ng5+ Kg6 {Black goes forward to g6, since ...Kg8 runs into Qh5.} 16. Qg4 {But after Qg4 Black's position is hopeless. Discovered check like Ne6+ or even grabbing the knight on h4 is very powerful here. [%cal Rg4h4,Rg5e6] [%csl Gg4,Rg6]}) 11. Bd3 Nxc3 {Black grabs a second pawn, unaware of the danger in the position. Qe1 or Qd2 would skewer the knights and win a piece, but even better is the Greek gift sacrifice! Once again, Black's queenside development is non-existent, which indicates that a sacrifice on the other side of the board is likely sound. [%csl Rh7,Ra8,Rc8,Rd8]} (11... Nf6 {In 34% of the games, the knight retreats to f6 here. Black's loose knight on a5 gives us the opportunity to activate our queen with tempo. [%csl Ra5]} 12. Qa4 b6 {Black supports the knight. The real purpose of Qa4 was to swing the queen over to the kingside to support our attack. [%csl Gb6,Ga5]} 13. Qh4 h6 {Black prevents us from adding pressure to the knight by playing Bg5... [%csl Rg5]} 14. Bxh6 {...so let's play Bxh6 instead! Black cannot recapture on h6, since they cannot bring enough forces from the queenside to defend their weak king. [%cal Gh4h6,Rg7h6] [%csl Rg8]}) (11... Nc5 {Nearly 30% of the time, Black brings the knight to c5 and tries to get our bishop from there. What might the best move be, considering Black's poor queenside development? [%csl Rd3]} 12. Bxh7+ Kxh7 {Bxh7+ works here as well! Black recaptures, and now we can play the typical Greek gift ideas.} 13. Ng5+ Kg6 {Black steps forward, since ...Kg8 walks into Qh5. We will now bring the queen into the attack. From g4, it threatens a strong discovered check.} 14. Qg4 d6 {Black opens up an attack on our queen... [%cal Rc8g4] [%csl Rg4]} (14... f5 {Black tries to attack our queen. We can just retreat it to g3 and the threat of Ne6+ is still relevant. [%csl Rg4]} 15. Qg3 {There is no good way for Black to prevent massive material loss from the knight and queen's discovered attacks. If Black tries to run with ...Kf6, we can play Be3 and continue involving pieces into the attack. We also always have the fork with Nh7+ in our back pocket. Black is toast. [%cal Gg5h7,Gc1e3,Ge3d4,Gg5e6,Rg6f6]}) 15. Ne6+ {...but after Ne6+, there will be a checkmate on the next move! [%cal Rg4g7] [%csl Rg7]}) 12. Bxh7+ Kxh7 {Black captures the bishop. Be careful not to play Ng5+? here, as Black can sacrifice their queen with ...Qxg5! because our queen is still hanging. Instead, let's get our queen out of danger with tempo, and involve her in the attack.} 13. Qd3+ Kg8 {Black retreats the king. We can now bring the knight in to threaten a checkmate on h7.} 14. Ng5 {If Black goes for ...f5 or ...g6, we can shift the queen to h3 and threaten a checkmate from there. [%cal Gd3h7,Gd3h3,Gh3h7,Rf7f5,Rg7g6] [%csl Rh7]} *

[Chapter "The c3 and d4 Italian"]
[Study "Black Plays 7...Bxc3+"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/Italian Center Attack/01-03 The c3 and d4 Italian - Black Plays 7...Bxc3+.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{This study delves into yet another of Black's 7th moves, which is ...Bxc3. Let's get right into it!}
1. e4 e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3. Bc4 Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4. c3 Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported. [%csl Re4]} 5. d4 exd4 {As in the last two studies, Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6. cxd4 Bb4+ {Black gives a check on b4, and the line recommended here is to block with Nc3. [%csl Re1]} 7. Nc3 Bxc3+ {Previously we have looked at 7...O-O, but in this line, Black takes on c3, removing the defender of e4 and intending to grab the pawn. This is played in approximately 15% of games. [%cal Rf6e4]} 8. bxc3 Nxe4 {Grabbing some material here and breaking down some of White's center is most sensible as Black. In many positions, if White was allowed to play e4-e5 or d4-d5, it would be very annoying for Black. Typically in this opening, after we sacrifice the e-pawn for development, we CANNOT allow d7-d5, which would stabilize Black's position. Thus, we should play d5 ourselves.} 9. d5 Na5 {40% of the time, Black jumps to a5 and attacks our bishop on c4. This is very similar to the previous study where both players have inserted O-O. As before, we should retreat the bishop to d3 with tempo. [%csl Rc4]} (9... Ne7 {In close to 30% of the games, Black went for ...Ne7. Since Black hasn't castled here, the g7-pawn is unprotected, and our queen can find a nice square on d4, from where it attacks both the g7-pawn and the knight on e4.} 10. Qd4 Nf6 {Black saves both the knight and the pawn, but at the cost of another tempo that they aren't spending developing. White should continue developing actively with Bg5, threatening to cripple Black's pawn structure.} 11. Bg5 {There is no good way for Black to defend against Bxf6 without further contorting their position. White will often regain the pawn here with positional interest, since Black's kingside pawns will be weak, and their king will be unsafe. [%cal Gd4f6,Gg5f6,Ge1g1] [%csl Rf6]}) 10. Bd3 Nxc3 {Black goes for even more material and captures another pawn. In the previous study where both sides castled, we had the Greek gift sacrifice in this position. Here, on the other hand, it is good enough to play Qd2, skewering the knights. We don't mind blocking the dark-squared bishop for now, since it will be fianchettoed to b2 later anyway.} (10... Nf6 {The knight retreats back to f6 instead of grabbing the d5-pawn, which would give White active development. Here, we can simply castle.} 11. O-O O-O {Black castles as well, and the position has transposed into a line from the previous study.} 12. Qa4 {And we've arrived at an already familiar position, where we first attack the knight on a5 and next slide the queen to h4 to initiate a strong attack! [%cal Ga4h4,Gf3g5,Ra4a5] [%csl Rg8]}) 11. Qd2 Qe7+ {Black gives a check on e7. This is necessary to bring the queen to c5 to defend both knights. We should not trade queens and give up our active play, so we move the king to f1! Black still has problems dealing with their hanging knights. [%csl Re1]} 12. Kf1 Qc5 {Black covers the knights with the queen.} 13. Bb2 {And now we fianchetto the bishop to b2, adding another attacker to Black's c3-knight. The pawn on g7 is also very loose, and with Re1+ coming, White is doing great here. [%cal Ga1e1,Rb2c3,Rd2c3] [%csl Rc3,Rg7]} *

[Chapter "The c3 and d4 Italian"]
[Study "Black Plays 6...Bb6"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/Italian Center Attack/01-04 The c3 and d4 Italian - Black Plays 6...Bb6.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{Welcome to the fourth study! Here, Black abandons the plan of giving a check on b4 (6...Bb4+) and goes for ...Bb6, which is already a bad move. Let's check out how to punish it!}
1. e4 e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3. Bc4 Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4. c3 Nf6 {Black brings the knight out and pressures the e4-pawn. We can now expand with d4 as it is well-supported. [%csl Re4]} 5. d4 exd4 {As in the previous studies, Black trades, and we will recapture with the pawn.} 6. cxd4 Bb6 {In 16% of the games, the bishop retreats to b6, which already gives White some advantage. Nevertheless, it could get a bit tricky to play this correctly as White. In general, we should try to quickly roll Black over by advancing the pawn center; in that vein, the move here is d4-d5.} 7. d5 Ne7 {In 35% of the games, Black retreats the knight to e7. e4 is still hanging, but notice that our pieces defend d5 twice. Thus, we can continue gaining space and disrupting Black's setup with e5! [%csl Re4]} (7... Nxe4 {This happens very rarely (only in 5% of the games in the database), as Black is sacrificing a piece on c6 and aggressively hunting our f2-pawn. Black's attacking forces aren't enough, so we should simply accept the material. [%csl Rf2]} 8. dxc6 Nxf2 {Black delivers a fork. Now, it is time to counterattack, starting by getting the queen out of danger with tempo. [%csl Rd1,Rh1]} 9. Qe2+ Qe7 {Black blocks with the queen, as they don't want to lose the castling rights with ...Kf8. We can throw in another check here by capturing on d7, thus avoiding losing the pawn on c6. [%cal Gc6d7] [%csl Gc6]} 10. cxd7+ Bxd7 {The bishop recaptures. Remember that we are up significant material, so it is now very acceptable to trade queens. [%csl Re7]} 11. Qxe7+ Kxe7 {After Black recaptures, we can save the rook by attacking the knight and threatening to win two pieces for a rook. [%cal Gh1f1,Gf1f2,Ge1f2] [%csl Gf2]} 12. Rf1 {The position is a bit strange, but White is clearly on top with the extra piece. Natural development will continue with moves like Nc3 and Bg5, and as long as White doesn't blunder material, they should be able to easily take over the endgame. [%cal Gb1c3,Gc1g5] [%csl Rf2]}) 8. e5 Ng4 {Black doesn't passively retreat to g8, but jumps to g4 instead, adding another attacker to f2. It's time to push again! We don't have to worry about f2. Black doesn't have enough firepower to generate a successful attack. Meanwhile, d5-d6 opens up our light-squared bishop aimed at the weak f7-pawn. [%cal Rb6f2,Rg4f2] [%csl Rf2]} 9. d6 cxd6 {Black can ignore f2 for now and trades on d6. We'll gladly recapture with the pawn, and attack the knight yet again.} (9... Nxf2 {Black captures on f2, going for the fork. [%csl Rd1,Rh1]} 10. Qb3 {The queen-bishop battery creates a higher level threat against the f7-pawn. [%csl Gb3,Gc4,Rf7]} 10... Nxh1 {Black goes for the material.} 11. Bxf7+ {Check!} 11... Kf8 {The only move.} 12. Bg5 {And after Bg5, there is tremendous pressure against the e7-knight, ensuring that we'll win Black's queen! Our opponent's lack of coordination and development is yet again fatal. [%cal Ge5d6,Rd6e7,Rc7d6] [%csl Re7,Rd8,Rf8]}) (9... Bxf2+ {if ...Bxf2+...} 10. Kf1 {...Kf1 and suddently all of Black's pieces are loose! We'll win at least one of them. [%cal Gh2h3,Gd1e2] [%csl Rf2,Rg4,Re7]}) 10. exd6 Nc6 {Black returns the knight to c6. It's time to continue working to keep Black's king in the center. Qe2+! forces Black to surrender castling rights. [%csl Re8]} 11. Qe2+ Kf8 {Black sidesteps their king. It is finally time to castle, so we get the king into safety and also protect the pawn on f2. [%csl Rf2]} 12. O-O {If Black allows it, we can go for a tactical shot with Bxf7, since after Ng5+, Black's knight on g4 will be hanging. Regardless, Black's position is miserable due to their stranded king, lack of development, and terrible piece coordination. [%cal Gf1f2,Rc4f7,Rf3g5,Re2g4] [%csl Gf2,Rc8,Ra8,Rg4]} *

[Chapter "Sidelines After 3...Bc5"]
[Study "Black Plays 4...d6"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/Italian Center Attack/02-01 Sidelines After 3...Bc5 - Black Plays 4...d6.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{In Chapter 2, we analyze 3...Bc5 with other moves. In this study, the move 4...d6 that protects the e5-pawn will be explored.}
1. e4 e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3. Bc4 Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4. c3 d6 {A move played about 25% of the time is 4...d6 to lend e5 some more support. This move is significantly less active than ...Nf6, since Black is not pressuring our e-pawn as in other lines. d2-d4 is still the clear continuation here, expanding and breaking in the center. [%csl Gd6,Ge5]} 5. d4 exd4 {Most of the time you'll see Black simply trade pawns, but we don't mind seeing this. After our c3-pawn recaptures, we will have two strong pawns in the center.} (5... Bb6 {Less than 10% of games continue with ...Bb6; Black retreats the bishop and hopes to keep it exerting pressure on our dark squares. After 6.dxe5, we are almost always simply winning a pawn. Most games (about 80%) continue with 6...Nxe5 or 6...dxe5, but both of those moves are terrible, since after trading queens, either the e5 or f7 pawns drop at the end of the line. [%csl Rd4]} 6. dxe5 Bg4 {Bg4, the best move, is only played in around 13% of games in the database. The best move for White here is to chop on d6 and grab the extra pawn. Note that 6...Bg4 is only roughly equal after 7.exd6 Nf6!?, with very strange complications. The majority of players aren't aware of these dynamics. [%csl Rd6]} 7. exd6 cxd6 {Most players simply recapture on d6, not knowing d6 becomes a weakness and that they lose valuable time. White should now castle, bringing their king to safety and enjoying their extra pawn and better position.} 8. O-O Ne5 {Perhaps Black's only idea to create counterplay, but it doesn't work. 9.Be2 is also fine, but even better is throwing in a check while saving the bishop. [%csl Rc4,Rf3]} 9. Bb5+ Kf8 {If Black is stubborn and they don't want to block the check, they'll have to lose castling rights. Now, we can bring the bishop back to break the pin.} 10. Be2 {Up a pawn with a better position, all we must do now is finish development. Na3 and Nc2 is a good maneuver to then pivot to e3. [%cal Gb1a3,Ga3c2,Gc2e3]}) 6. cxd4 Bb4+ {Check! This is played half of the time in the database, but it isn't that good. We block and develop with Nc3, and in this move order, Black has not played ...Nf6 and thus cannot win our e-pawn. [%csl Re1]} (6... Bb6 {This move here is played in a more favorable move order. The reason is that Black has inserted ...exd4 and so they won't be left with the problem of dxe5. We'll play Bb5, which threatens to win the pinned c6-knight with d5.} 7. Bb5 Bd7 {Black unpins. Now Nc3 develops further and supports our center. The short-term plan is simple: h3 and castling.} 8. Nc3 Nge7 {Developing the knight to e7 helps Black a little because if we play e5 it won't attack their knight like in the ...Nf6 line. We castle.} (8... Nf6 {The knight can go on f6. But since our knight already defends e4, we can simply castle. [%csl Re4]} 9. O-O O-O {Black castles as well.} 10. h3 {And here we prevent ...Bg4. We want to play moves Re1 and maybe even e5 to attack the f6-knight. We've got a clear advantage thanks to our two central pawns. [%cal Gf1e1,Gb5c4,Ge4e5] [%csl Rg4]}) 9. O-O O-O {They do the same.} 10. h3 {But again, we prevent ...Bg4 and we will continue with Re1 and moves like Be3. [%cal Gf1e1,Gc1e3] [%csl Rg4]}) 7. Nc3 Bg4 {The bishop pins our f3-knight. We can simply castle here and start using our lead in development to cause problems for Black. [%csl Rf3,Rd1]} (7... Nf6 {It's quite possible that we could have a transposition with this move order. We'll castle.} 8. O-O O-O {If the main line is avoided and Black castles, we'll go 9.Bg5.} 9. Bg5 {This pin is powerful because of our Nd5 and Qd3 ideas. We've got moves to increase the pressure, but also to develop all our pieces and support our center. Black will have a tough time fighting our central pawn duo. [%cal Gc3d5,Gd1d3] [%csl Rf6]}) 8. O-O Nf6 {Black develops the knight and pressures e4. The position seems stable for Black, but it actually is not. Now Nd5! is a tricky move: we can attack the bishop, and all the continuations favor White. [%csl Re4]} 9. Nd5 Nxd5 {For instance if Black trades, we open the e-file with tempo. More importantly, once the c6 knight is deflected, we will have Qa4+, winning the bishop. [%cal Gd1a4,Ga4e8,Ga4b4,Re4d5] [%csl Gb4,Rc6]} 10. exd5 Bxf3 {Black may try and take on f3, but we won't recapture with the queen.} 11. gxf3 {The double isolated pawns are a bit ugly, but they are a small price to pay for White's imminent material gain. [%cal Gd1a4] [%csl Re8,Rb4]} *

[Chapter "Sidelines After 3...Bc5"]
[Study "Black Plays 4...h6 and 4...Qf6"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/Italian Center Attack/02-02 Sidelines After 3...Bc5 - Black Plays 4...h6 and 4...Qf6.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{For this second study, we have to cover 4...h6 and 4...Qf6. Combined, these moves occur less than 10% in the database, but it could be useful to know how to apply our Italian ideas even against such strange moves.}
1. e4 e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares.} 3. Bc4 Bc5 {The bishops are developed to their typical Italian squares, and now White will commence the plan of immediately playing c2-c3 and d2-d4.} 4. c3 h6 {This is a terrible move. The Fried Liver was already stopped, so 4...h6 is a waste of time. Still, 5% of players, which is about 1 million games, have gone for this. Because Black's play has been so slow, we have the flashy idea of b2-b4! and then b4-b5, simply dislodging the defense of e5 and winning a pawn. [%cal Gb2b4,Gb4b5,Gf3e5] [%csl Ge5]} (4... Qf6 {Less than 5% of players go for this, but many beginners like to bring the queen out to be aggressive and threaten Scholar's Mate patterns. Additionally, ...Qf6 adds an additional defender to the d4 square, so it looks like our idea of d2-d4 is prevented. But in fact, we should still play it! [%csl Rf2,Rd4]} 5. d4 exd4 {The most popular line is this trade. It's also the best since Black has to try to break our pawns apart. And now, a key move that keeps reappearing in this opening: 6.e5!, setting up a trap.} 6. e5 Nxe5 {Most players fall for the trap. 6... Nxe5 is a mistake. We have 7.Qe2, pinning the knight.} (6... Qg6 {Moving away from the e5-pawn is the best move, but now we can recapture on d4 and consolidate our center. [%csl Rg2]} 7. cxd4 Bb4+ {Check! But we can simply block. With Nc3, we are getting our ideal Italian setup: knights on f3 and c3, bishop on c4 and two pawns in the center! [%csl Re1]} 8. Nc3 Qxg2 {This capture is a blunder. We start with Rg1, of course.} 9. Rg1 {We have a forced win.} 9... Qh3 {The queen goes to h3.} 10. Bxf7+ {Black's position was already bad, but now they lose another pawn and castling rights as well. The bishop is tactically defended by Ng5+, forking the king and queen. Once White consolidates, they will have an easy game with the strong center, semi-open g-file, and easy development. [%cal Gf3g5,Re8f7] [%csl Rf7,Rh3,Re8]}) 7. Qe2 d6 {If they try to defend it, do you remember the c3-pawn? [%csl Gc3]} 8. cxd4 {We are going to go up material. White is completely winning. [%csl Rc5,Re5]}) 5. b4 Bb6 {9 out of ten games continue like this. The bishop wants to stay active. But the point of the queenside expansion is to also attack the knight, which defends e5. [%csl Re5]} 6. b5 Na5 {If ...Nce7, simply Nxe5. So Black attacks our bishop instead; if we move it to safety, the position is merely equal. It is critical that we make this b4-b5 operation be worth it. Notice that Black's pieces are underdeveloped and offside. It is time for a sacrifice! [%csl Rc4]} 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 {Black is lost. Our attack plays itself, starting by grabbing the e5 pawn with tempo. [%csl Re5]} 8. Nxe5+ Ke6 {The king moves forward. Qh5 now defends the knight and threatens a check with checkmate to follow.} 9. Qh5 Qf6 {The queen prevents the check on f5.} 10. d4 {And we can push d4 to defend our knight and threaten more checks when the time is right. Black is lost. [%cal Gd4d5] [%csl Re6]} *

[Chapter "Move 3 Sidelines"]
[Study "Black Plays 3...h6"]
[Video "/downloader/downloads/Italian Center Attack/03-01 Move 3 Sidelines - Black Plays 3...h6.mp4"]
[Orientation "white"]

{In this study, we'll see what to do if Black plays 3...h6 to protect their g5-square. This move isn't good this early, because it wastes time that they should use in developing pieces.}
1. e4 e5 {Once again, 1.e4 e5.} 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Both knights are developed to their natural squares. We play Bc4 here, to enter the Italian.} 3. Bc4 h6 {3...h6 is surprisingly played 13% of the time here. At such an early stage of the game, this includes more than 32 million games! Still, if the idea is to avoid a Fried Liver at all costs, 3...Bc5 is better. Because Black has essentially wasted a move, we should strike immediately in the center with d2-d4; there is no reason to prepare it with c3 here. [%cal Gf3g5] [%csl Rg5]} 4. d4 exd4 {About 70% of the games in the database continue like this, with Black opening the position. We should recapture with the knight.} (4... d6 {About 12% of games continue with 4...d6. Black defends e5, but the simple capture on e5 is good. [%cal Gd4e5] [%csl Ge5]} 5. dxe5 Bg4 {Black recaptures on e5 in 80% of games. In the previous 3...Bc5 lines, we saw that recapturing the pawn on e5 is lost due to Qxd8+ and Bxf7! ideas. The only move to save the game is once again ...Bg4, and even then, the defense is not obvious. In less than 20% of games, players managed to find this move. Obviously, we can now take on d6 and be up a pawn.} 6. exd6 Bxd6 {The bishop recaptures. It's more active on the d6-square in this line because it helps control the center. We should start by forcing Black to making a choice with the light-squared bishop.} 7. h3 Bh5 {If they keep the pin, that's fine. Our plan will be to play g4 later. First, though, we can pin their knight to avoid Ne5. [%cal Gg2g4] [%csl Gc6]} 8. Bb5 Ne7 {Black supports their knight with the other one. We have some time to develop here. Nc3 supports our center.} 9. Nc3 O-O {Black castles. ...Ne5 is enabled. It is time to play g4. There won't be significant weaknesses that Black will be able to take advantage of, especially since we haven't castled yet. This expansion lets us play actively and aggressively. [%cal Gc6e5]} 10. g4 Bg6 {The bishop retreats to g6. There's some pressure on e4, but it's currently defended. [%csl Re4]} 11. Be3 {We're going to play Qe2, Bd3 in some variations if we need to defend e4, and castle queenside. Rg1 is also an idea to push our kingside pawns. White stands better due to the space advantage and the extra pawn. [%cal Gd1e2,Gb5d3,Ge1c1,Gh1g1]}) 5. Nxd4 Nxd4 {If this capture happens, we've got everything we wanted in the position. After 6.Qxd4, we have a massive lead in development and unopposed central control.} (5... Bc5 {This move loses on the spot.} 6. Bxf7+ {With the idea of Qh5+. If Black doesn't take the bishop, we can simply trade down. White will be up a pawn and will enjoy hunting Black's king, which can no longer castle. [%cal Ge8f7,Gd1h5] [%csl Re8,Rf7,Rc5]}) (5... Nf6 {5...Nf6 attacks e4. We can defend naturally with 6.Nc3. [%csl Re4]} 6. Nc3 Bb4 {Black pins our knight, and thus rethreatens ...Nxe4. We can start by trading the other knights and relieving some tension. [%cal Rf6e4] [%csl Rc3,Re4]} 7. Nxc6 bxc6 {The b-pawn recaptures. We should castle now. With our king in safety, it'll be harder to win a pawn on e4.} 8. O-O Bxc3 {Black takes. They may feel like they have to at this point. After recapturing, our plan is to play e5 to dislodge the knight. It's dangerous for Black to take on e4. [%cal Gb2c3,Ge4e5] [%csl Gc3,Gf6]} 9. bxc3 Nxe4 {If they take on e4, we have some ways to create problems.} 10. Ba3 {We start taking control of the dark squares around the king. We also stop them from castling here. The best continuation for Black is 10...d5 11.Bb3 Nd6. But in that case we can attack c6 with 12.Ba4. [%cal Gd7d5,Gc4b3,Gb3a4] [%csl Re7,Rf8,Rc6]}) 6. Qxd4 d6 {Black opens up their light-squared bishop. Since our position has already been opened thanks to our first moves and d4-pawn break, we can simply continue development with Nc3.} 7. Nc3 Nf6 {Black develops the knight. We can continue developing our pieces with Bf4.} 8. Bf4 Be7 {Black prepares to castle. We can castle queenside here and use our dominating position to start an attack! [%csl Gc4,Gd4,Gc3,Gf4]} 9. O-O-O O-O {Black castles, but their king is now on a lonely side of the board.} 10. h4 {10.e5 or 10.f3 were also options, but I like 10.h4 to attack. This may make many players with Black nervous, and spend some time thinking of how to defend. f3 is an idea that we could also go for later, and Qd2 to sacrifice on h6. [%cal Gf2f3,Gd4d2] [%csl Rh6]} *

